.TH MMAP_READAMP 1 "September 2026" "ebpf-syscall" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mmap_readamp \- measure file and block IO read amplification with eBPF
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mmap_readamp
.RB [ \-\-pid " PID" ]
.RB [ \-\-dur " SECONDS" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mmap_readamp
compares several witnesses of an IO workload: file-backed demand faults,
page-cache insertions, read and write syscall intent, iomap direct IO, and
physical block requests.  It prints per-file and per-device counts, byte
totals, request-size histograms, and IO-unit amplification.
.PP
The useful-byte denominator is workload-specific and must be supplied by the
application or another trace.  A fault-byte count is not automatically equal
to useful application data.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-pid " PID"
Restrict process-scoped probes to one thread-group ID.  A value of zero or an
omitted option observes all processes where the probe supports it.
.TP
.BI \-\-dur " seconds"
Stop after the given duration.  Otherwise stop with SIGINT or SIGTERM.
.SH OUTPUT
Page-cache insertion is a pre-filesystem-submission observation, not proof of
device completion.  O_DIRECT bypasses the page cache.  Block request records
show physical device IO but no longer carry a file inode.  Use the separate
witnesses together when making a read-amplification claim.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Requires root privileges, BTF for the running kernel, and kernel functions
compatible with the generated eBPF skeleton.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kvio (1),
.BR nvme_tp_monitor (1)
